  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe region remains in a relative lull of activity between\novernight/early morning activity and developing convection to\nthe west, where a couple of Severe Thunderstorm Warnings are\nalready in effect. The new activity is associated with a vort \nmax/MCV over northwest Ohio. Ahead of this, a good deal of \nsunshine is allowing for a buildup of instability, and the HREF \nsuggests that 1500-2500 J/kg of surface-based CAPE is likely by \n18Z, mainly south of I-80. Other convective parameters look best\nfrom I-80 on south, roughly matching with the SPC Slight Risk \narea. In particular, a pocket of 25-30 knots of 0-6km shear is \nmost likely in this region, supporting multicells with mainly a \ndamaging gust potential where cold pools help consolidate into \nshort lines. Lengthening low-level hodographs and sufficient \n0-1km SRH continue to suggest a low-end tornado threat for any \nESE-moving storms. \n\nAlong with this the threat of flash flooding will continue to be\nmonitored, especially in areas that got heavier rain overnight.\nThis includes areas in eastern Ohio south of I-70, as well as a\nstrip extending from New Philadelphia ENE towards Butler County.\nAlthough storms should be fairly progressive overall, the\nenvironment remains highly supportive for efficient rainfall\nproduction. The 12Z KPIT sounding recorded a precipitable water\nmeasurement of 2.15 inches, which is the 9th-highest recorded \nat the site since 1952. 2+ inch values remain in place through\nthis evening, along with 11k to 13k warm-cloud depths. The Flood\nWatch will be maintained through tonight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nMost of the convection should pull off to the east by 02Z-03Z,\nalthough a few showers/storms may linger behind the main line as\nanother shortwave trough crosses in the mid-level zonal flow.\nOne last ripple in that flow crosses on Monday, leading to\nshowers and storms mainly south of Pittsburgh. Convective\nparameters are unfavorable for severe weather with this\nactivity. Also, declining precipitable water values point to a\ndecreasing risk for heavy rainfall. The Flood Watch expires at 8\nAM Monday, and an extension may not be needed.\n\nHigh pressure building in from the Great Lakes should end \nprecipitation by Mon evening. Dry weather is then favored until \nWednesday, when the next front could potentially arrive, \nalthough this timing could change. At this time, severe weather\nand flooding risks appear low with this system, as supported by\nlonger-range machine learning guidance.",
